---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';
import { absoluteUrl } from '../lib/site';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="About László Viktor: a hobbyist who writes Rust for the terminal, and writes down how he solved things."
  jsonLd={{
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Person',
    name: SITE.author,
    givenName: 'Viktor',
    familyName: 'László',
    url: absoluteUrl('/about/'),
    sameAs: [SITE.links.github],
  }}
>
  <PageHead
    eyebrow="About"
    title="Hi, I'm Viktor"
    lede="László Viktor, from Hungary. This is what I do with my free time."
  />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      By day I have a day job. After it, I write software for fun, mostly in Rust, mostly for the
      terminal. A fair share of my free time goes into waiting for things to compile, and I've made
      peace with it: it's not frozen, it's just Rust. (Alt-Tab is my main sport.)
    </p>
    <p>
      The pattern is always the same. I learn how something works, then I can't rest until I've
      built one. That's how I ended up with a radio in my terminal.
    </p>

    <h2>What I'm building</h2>
    <p>
      My biggest project is <a class="external" href={SITE.links.sdrtop}>sdrtop</a>, a terminal
      monitor for software-defined radios. It shows spectrum and waterfall, measures things
      instead of guessing them, and runs keyboard-only, even over SSH on a modest machine. I test
      it against a HackRF One, an RTL-SDR and a tinySA Ultra.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is never almost done. I said that before 0.6.0 too, and then Bluetooth happened. Before
      the radio phase it was homelab dashboards and containers, and whatever comes next will
      probably end up here as well.
    </p>

    <h2>What you'll find here</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Technical write-ups:</strong> how I solved something, the logic behind it, and how
        it could have been done differently. The roads not taken are often the more interesting
        ones.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dev logs</strong> with the dead ends left in, because that's where I actually learn
        things.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Everything around the code:</strong> programming in general, some philosophy, and
        the occasional personal thing.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Expect opinions with error bars. If I'm guessing, I'll say so, and if I got something wrong,
      I'd much rather be corrected than stay confidently wrong.
    </p>

    <h2>Say hi</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a class="external" href={SITE.links.github}>GitHub: {SITE.handle}</a></li>
      <li><a href="mailto:viktor.laszlo92@protonmail.com">viktor.laszlo92@protonmail.com</a></li>
      <li><a href="/rss.xml">RSS feed</a>, if you like new posts to come to you</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The blog itself is a static site with no trackers and no cookie banner. The source is on
      <a class="external" href="https://github.com/musithang/musithang.github.io">GitHub</a>, and
      the <a href="/projects/blog/">devlog</a> covers how it was built.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
